      "rev_n_2_720384_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_720384_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_720384",
        "thought_text": "I understand the problem and the error in the previous solution. The main issue is that the code doesn't correctly handle the case where all strict prefixes are not correct parenthesis sequences. Let's improve the reasoning to address this:\n\nInstead of replacing all '?' with '(' in the first pass and then all remaining '?' with ')', we should try to construct a valid sequence that satisfies the conditions. We can do this by maintaining a balance counter and greedily assigning '(' or ')' based on the current balance and the remaining characters. This approach ensures that we create a valid sequence while trying to make all strict prefixes invalid.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_720384_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_044bc14866",
        "created_at": 1794529501.4874396
      },
      "rev_n_1_b4833b_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_b4833b_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_b4833b",
        "thought_text": "I understand the issue. The current approach of replacing '?' with ')' when open_needed > 0 is incorrect. This can lead to invalid sequences where some strict prefixes become correct. Instead, we should replace '?' with '(' when open_needed <= 0 to ensure all strict prefixes remain incorrect parenthesis sequences. This change will make the solution correct while maintaining the required properties of the output string.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_b4833b_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_2f1e6b8a57",
        "created_at": 1794529528.000534
      },
      "rev_n_3_6d2dbb_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_6d2dbb_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_6d2dbb",
        "thought_text": "Replace '?' from right to left, prioritizing closing parentheses, but ensure that the number of closing parentheses never exceeds the number of open parentheses at any point. This approach guarantees that all strict prefixes are not correct parenthesis sequences while maintaining the overall sequence as a correct parenthesis sequence.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_6d2dbb_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_15d3beb59f",
        "created_at": 1794529555.9194996
